From: EglantinePS5
Subject: [PATCH] up: EDEN_UP_GPU_BADALLOC_BACKSTOP=1: a std::bad_alloc out of one engine method call on the GPU threads is caught, counted, the heap trimmed and the next method runs; off as before

T178a. With the switch (exact "1"; off without it): the split's back (GpuSplit::BackLoop runs
each ring entry through ExecuteCaught) and the single-thread path (DmaPusher::CallMethod and
CallMultiMethod, the engine call) catch a std::bad_alloc from one entry / one engine call, print
EDEN_UP_GPU_BADALLOC (where=back|single|dma, method, count, KiB eden_heap_trim gave back; the
first 16, then each power of two) and go on with the next. The back skips the entry by the size
Execute would have returned (EntryWords: the same tests, the constant-buffer and draw.begin runs
included). MaxwellDMA catches inside Launch instead, so a lost copy still releases its semaphore
(a game waiting on it would otherwise wait for ever).

What a dropped call can leave inconsistent: the method's own work is lost (a draw is missing for
that frame, a copy or upload does not land, so a surface can show old bytes until the game writes
it again), and whatever it changed before the throw stays (registers, cache bookkeeping, dirty
flags cleared for state it never bound). Limited by: register writes still queued for the engine
(its method sink) are dropped with the call (a throw in ConsumeSink would otherwise run them
again), a Maxwell3D marks all of its dirty flags (the next draw binds everything again), a
deferred function (Call entry) leaves the engine alone. Locks are RAII and let go while the
exception leaves. After EDEN_UP_GPU_BADALLOC_MAX (default 64) caught throws the next is not caught
and the session ends as before. Off: each entry runs through Execute and each call bare, as T177
(one well predicted test of a static bool).

New file video_core/gpu_badalloc_backstop.h (header only). None of the touched files is a
translation id input and no name is added to TranslationSwitches: KEEPS THE STORE. Not checked on
the console.

